# Rebuilt as `.gitignore` on every direnv load by .envrcs/.envrc.gitignore:
# this template, then the untracked `.gitignore.local` (your own rules) if
# present. Edit those, never `.gitignore`. A repo that keeps a `.gitignore`
# of its own does not track this template, and nothing is rebuilt.
# Anchored: unanchored, `.gitignore` would also ignore every nested one.
/.gitignore
/.gitignore.local
/.gitignore.orig
/.gitignore.tmp.*

# Build products of the user layer: .direnv is direnv's own cache, .venv is
# created by `uv sync`, /result* by a nix build. The last two are anchored
# with a leading slash: nix only ever writes them at the root, and unanchored
# they would swallow any directory named result/ anywhere in the tree.
.direnv
.venv
/result
/result-*

# Local direnv fragments, auto-created from their templates
.envrcs/.envrc.secrets
.envrcs/.envrc.user

# Per-user dotenv fragments (third layer) and plaintext secret sources.
# Plaintext never gets committed, so the wildcard is deliberately broad.
.envrcs/.env.*
.env
*.env

# Tracked exceptions, applied after the rules above:
#  - the templates, which the wildcard would otherwise hide from a fresh clone
#  - sops-encrypted bundles: an encrypted artifact is safe to commit, and
#    committing it is what lets a clone reach a working environment
!.envrcs/.env.*.template
!*.sops-encrypted
